  <!-- WHAT MAKES US DIFFERENT -->
  <div class="about-section">
    <h2>What Makes Our Calculators Different</h2>
    <p>Most free calculator sites use shortcut math. Here is specifically what we do differently:</p>

    <h3>Real Progressive Tax Brackets — Not Flat Rates</h3>
    <p>Our Ohio payroll calculators apply the actual <strong>2026 IRS Publication 15</strong> progressive federal brackets (10% through 37%) based on filing status and annualized income. Ohio state tax uses the current <strong>Ohio Department of Taxation</strong> brackets: 0% up to $26,050, 2.75% up to $100,000, and 3.75% above. We never use a single flat percentage like "tax is approximately 22%."</p>

    <h3>Municipal Tax for 600+ Ohio Cities</h3>
    <p>Ohio is one of only a handful of US states with widespread municipal income taxes. Our Ohio paycheck calculators support Columbus (2.5%), Akron (2.5%), Toledo (2.25%), Dayton (2.25%), Cleveland (2.0%), and Cincinnati (1.8%) — plus a custom rate field for any other Ohio city.</p>

    <h3>Verified Market Benchmarks</h3>
    <p>Real estate yield benchmarks are sourced from Global Property Guide's quarterly US surveys (national average: 6.71% gross yield, Q2 2026). SBA loan rates are cross-referenced against current FRED economic data and SBA lender bulletins. We cite sources and update regularly.</p>

    <h3>Standard Amortization Math</h3>
    <p>Loan calculators use the industry-standard formula: <code style="background:#f8fafc;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:4px;font-size:.9rem;">M = P × [r(1+r)^n] / [(1+r)^n − 1]</code> — the same formula your bank uses. Every amortization schedule shows the exact principal and interest split for every payment.</p>
  </div>

